WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:09.890
Phil: Where I work, we have a school-wide
positive behavior support policy,

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:14.660
which as I said earlier you really do
see the direct correlation when that's

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.920
implemented well and a consistent
approach is important as well as.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:24.020
A reward system, first of all,  we use
Class Dojo, which is used, I've seen used

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.970
in quite a lot of other schools actually.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:34.635
And to reinforce that we have four/five
categories: so it's independent,

00:00:34.985 --> 00:00:39.735
safe and sensible, being considerate
to others and then, so aiming

00:00:39.735 --> 00:00:41.445
high, being prepared and engaged.

00:00:41.655 --> 00:00:45.165
So, five or six, but there's a
couple sort of grouped in together.

00:00:45.765 --> 00:00:51.785
So when we see those behaviors, we
make it clear and you know, that

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:53.385
positive behavior has happened.

00:00:53.685 --> 00:00:57.165
Like, "oh, you held the door open for
me there, that was really considerate".

00:00:57.345 --> 00:01:01.800
And then would reinforce it with
like, with a considerate dojo points.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:05.370
And then the points, as I said, the
points win prizes and then they have a

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:09.830
dojo shop so the pupils at the end of the
week will go and spend that dojo prizes.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:16.400
Zoe T: You would have to use limit setting
as part of your interaction, let's say a

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.230
school, if you're working with children.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.245
But, I wouldn't necessarily say that
that has to be done in a behaviorist way.

00:01:23.275 --> 00:01:26.414
Again, you can approach that from a
kind of developmental perspective.

00:01:27.075 --> 00:01:32.265
Kerry: I have a weekly meeting with a PBS
practitioner at school where we talk over

00:01:32.265 --> 00:01:35.985
any incidents that had happened in the
week, and we really try and break down and

00:01:35.985 --> 00:01:38.145
get to the communication of that behavior.

00:01:38.175 --> 00:01:43.485
So what need was being met by that
behavior and how can we support it

00:01:43.485 --> 00:01:45.465
to happen in a more functional way?

00:01:46.995 --> 00:01:55.005
So for instance, one young person
will pull hair quite frequently.

00:01:55.544 --> 00:01:57.524
And when we really broke down
the communication of that

00:01:57.524 --> 00:01:59.475
behavior, it was for attention.

00:02:00.464 --> 00:02:03.134
So it's then about making sure
that the people who are supporting

00:02:03.134 --> 00:02:06.345
them are giving them that
hundred percent one-to-one focus.

00:02:06.945 --> 00:02:10.365
And then we saw a decrease in the
hair pulling behaviors because

00:02:10.365 --> 00:02:11.415
we were meeting that need.

00:02:12.424 --> 00:02:16.665
Phil: As I mentioned earlier, you're
not, instead of being punitive, either

00:02:16.685 --> 00:02:21.620
taking things away, you know, telling
someone off -  you really reinforce,

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:31.020
recognizing and reinforcing the positive
behaviors that are desirable, not only

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:35.550
in school, but you know, in life which
I think is also important, linking it

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:40.710
all back to life outside of the school.

